<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chilean War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chilean War of Independence was the struggle of Chilean patriots against Spanish royalist rule, from the first national junta of 1810 to the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> in 1826. After royalist forces reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1814, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> crossed the mountains, won at Chacabuco (1817) and Maipú (1818), and independence was declared in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chilean-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Chilean War of Independence" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan E. Casanova P · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captura-de-Chilo%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of the crisis of the Spanish monarchy after Napoleonic forces captured King Ferdinand VII in 1808. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, one of the smallest and poorest colonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, Governor Francisco García Carrasco alienated the criollo elite with his harsh manner, a corruption case known as the Scorpion scandal and the arrest without due process of prominent citizens suspected of favouring a junta. He was forced to resign on 16 July 1810, and his successor, the 82-year-old criollo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mateo-de-toro-zambrano">Mateo de Toro Zambrano</a>, called an open cabildo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> for 18 September 1810, which set up the First Junta, sworn loyal to Ferdinand VII.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chilean historians divide the period into the Patria Vieja (1810-1814), the Reconquista (1814-1817) and the Patria Nueva (1817-1823). The fighting itself began in 1812. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a>, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, took power in two coups at the end of 1811, issued the provisional Constitution of 1812 and founded the Instituto Nacional and the National Library. The Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a>, sent expeditions south, and on 1 and 2 October 1814 the royalists under Mariano Osorio crushed the patriots at Rancagua, where only 500 of 5,000 survived.</p><p>The persecution that followed, led by Vicente San Bruno, persuaded even moderates that only independence would do. Exiles gathered in Mendoza, while the lawyer Manuel Rodríguez waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign. After Chacabuco on 12 February 1817, O'Higgins became Supreme Director. A royalist force surprised the patriots at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-cancha-rayada">Second Battle of Cancha Rayada</a> on 18 March 1818, but San Martín's victory at Maipú on 5 April ended the threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. A merciless irregular war in the south, the Guerra a muerte, lasted until the band of Vicente Benavides was destroyed in 1822.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>O'Higgins built a navy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a>, who took the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a> in 1820 and carried San Martín's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, whose independence was secured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> fell in 1826 under Ramón Freire, the last royalists in Araucanía in 1827, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised Chilean independence in 1844. The wars damaged the wheat trade, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> loan of 1822 left a debt that was not regularised until the 1840s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chilean War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
